1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,720
 In this video, I'll be talking about structures.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:08,160
 I'll explain what they are, how to define, create, and access data within structures,

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:14,040
 and a little bit about anonymous structures.

4
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:16,000
 Structures allow data to be stored in groups.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,280
 These are similar to a class in other programming languages.

6
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:23,760
 Each data point in the structure is called a field, and storing data in groups is usually

7
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:28,560
 more efficient than storing them on their own, both for running your program and for

8
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:30,880
 reading or writing your code.

9
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:34,360
 It is possible to associate functionality with structures, sort of like writing a method

10
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:38,640
 for a class, which further helps organize your code and your data.

11
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:45,320
 We won't be covering that in this video, but it will be in a future lesson.

12
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,440
 Let's take a look at how to define a structure.

13
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:54,000
 We'll use the type keyword, followed by the name of the structure, and then finally, the

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 struct keyword.

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,520
 We'll have some curly braces, and within the curly braces we'll have our fields.

16
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,160
 This field is named field, and it is a type of string.

17
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:08,960
 Here we have an A and a B, and both of those are an int.

18
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:13,400
 So as you can see, this is similar to the function parameters when you write functions.

19
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:20,240
 Let's take a look at how to instantiate a structure.

20
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:25,840
 We'll be working with the sample struct that we just created, and to make one in memory,

21
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:32,120
 we can create a variable, create an assign, use the name of our structure, the curly braces,

22
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,280
 and then each field of the structure in order.

23
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:41,160
 We need to make sure that we have the fields in order for this example, because we don't

24
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:43,160
 list the names of the fields.

25
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:46,760
 On the second example, however, we are listing out the names of the field, so they can

26
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:50,440
 be in any order you like, but you would generally want to follow the same order as a struct

27
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:51,440
 itself.

28
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:58,480
 That way, if you make updates, it's easier to change it.

29
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:02,080
 Just like with variables, if you try to create a structure and don't supply any data,

30
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:03,640
 the fields will have default values.

31
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:07,800
 So here on this first example, we're creating a sample structure, and we have curly

32
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:09,760
 braces with nothing in between.

33
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:12,640
 This means that all the fields will have default values.

34
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:19,360
 So the field field will just have an empty string, A and B will both be equal to zero.

35
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:23,960
 It still will be valid to use this sample structure, because default values will populate

36
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:26,160
 when necessary.

37
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:30,080
 On this second example, we're creating another sample structure, and we're setting A equal

38
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:31,080
 to five.

39
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:38,680
 So A will now be five, but field and B will have their default values set.

40
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:41,480
 When we're working with structures, we'll need to be able to access the fields within

41
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,880
 the structures, both for reading and writing.

42
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:47,160
 To do so, we use dot notation.

43
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:52,040
 So we'll have the variable name that has the structure, A dot, and then the field name

44
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:55,280
 that we want to access.

45
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:00,720
 In this data example on the bottom, we created a sample structure, and the field is word,

46
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:03,520
 A is one, and B is two.

47
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:09,720
 So if we create the word variable and do the structure name dot field, then this word variable

48
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:15,120
 will contain the word word.

49
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:25,800
 And the same goes for A and B right here, A will be one, and B will be two.

50
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:29,760
 We can modify the structure by reassigning the values.

51
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:33,400
 So we take our data that we created, which is the structure here.

52
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:39,000
 We access the field field, and we use an equal sign and set a new value.

53
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:49,840
 So field will now have hello in it, A will have ten, and B will have twenty.

54
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:53,960
 It's possible to create anonymous or inline structures inside of a function.

55
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:57,000
 This is useful when you're working with library functions or when shipping data across

56
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,040
 a network.

57
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:03,520
 What this allows you to do is easily define the data structure where you need it, instead

58
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:09,480
 of having it in a different file or somewhere else across the source code.

59
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:15,360
 Here's an example of some anonymous structures.

60
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,880
 To create them, we use the var keyword, just like a variable.

61
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:23,880
 We give it a name, and we use the keyword struct afterward.

62
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:31,960
 We then have our curly braces, and we have our field names, and our types for the fields.

63
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:35,720
 And then we can access it just like we have been using the variable name, and then dot

64
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:39,720
 notation, and the fields that we want to access.

65
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:44,480
 On this second example here on the right, we use the create and assign to create a sample.

66
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:50,080
 We then use the keyword struct, our curly braces, the field names that we want, and then

67
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:54,360
 additional curly braces to define what's going to be in those fields.

68
00:04:54,360 --> 00:04:59,720
 So in this example, we have field A and B. Hello, we're going to field.

69
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:06,280
 And then we'll go into A and two will go into B. The main difference between this one

70
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:11,080
 on the left and the one on the right is the one on the left will have default values

71
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,600
 if you don't supply any.

72
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:18,120
 So as soon as you create the variable with your var keyword, all of these fields will immediately

73
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:24,000
 have default values, and then later on you can assign them to whatever you need.

74
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,960
 The one on the right, however, has to declare these fields and assign them all at the same

75
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:31,600
 time, and you can't be missing any fields.

76
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:36,160
 So when you use the create and assign shorthand, you won't be able to have default values

77
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:38,040
 for any field infrastructure.

78
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:42,700
 Let's recap.

79
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:47,040
 Structures are used to group similar data, and these data points are called fields.

80
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:51,280
 Structures are defined using a type alias with the type keyword.

81
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:55,160
 Fields within the structure can be accessed using dot notation.

82
00:05:55,160 --> 00:06:00,520
 Any fields that are not present during instantiation are set to defaults.

83
00:06:00,520 --> 00:06:03,160
 In line, anonymous structures can be created within functions.

